figdown 0.1 block
# The §5 attributes an element cannot carry, because it draws no such
# thing: a boundary is never drawn (only its label exists), and typed blocks
# stack outside the scene with per-item dashes reserved for `conditional`
# fields. `band` left this list (`BAND-LABEL-STATUS`): it gained a mandatory
# label, and the text channel came with it.
#
# 0.3 (`PAINT-ORDER-CONSTRUCT`) took a whole ATTRIBUTE out of the carve-out, so the list
# is one shorter than it was. Until then every typed-block line below also
# wrote ` plane=base` — `bitfield`, `field`, `timing`, `signal`, `cell` and
# `table` each answered `<directive> does not take plane=`, and that refusal
# was the second half of the carve-out's reason (nothing to layer against,
# because the typed blocks stack outside the scene). `plane=` has now been
# withdrawn from the LANGUAGE: it is refused on every directive in every
# genre, so a `plane=` written here would no longer be evidence of anything
# directive-specific — it would be the language-wide withdrawal firing, and
# that is case 918's subject, not this one.
#
# What remains is the carve-out proper: `style=` on the three region openers,
# and the three §5 attributes `external` refuses. The three typed-block
# CHILDREN — `field`, `signal`, `cell` — were in the fixture only to be told
# `does not take plane=`; two of them are gone with the key, and `cell`
# stays, written clean, because `fill=` on a cell is accepted and the
# contrast is the point. Their own `style=` refusal is `STYLE-KEY-SCOPE`'s separate
# reasoned diagnostic and is pinned by 478-typed-block-style-retired.
external w "ext" fill=#ffffff stroke=#000000 style=dashed
group g "G"
node a "A" in=g
bitfield bf "B" style=dashed
timing wv "W" style=dotted
table t "T"
| h |
|---|
| 1 |
cell (1,1) fill=#fee2e2
table t2 "T2" style=solid
